    This page contains a hands-on tutorial to get you used to running PB3D to calculate 3-D ideal linear high-n MHD stability, and POST to post-process the results.
    
    Be sure to check out the \ref page_installation instruction first.
    
    \htmlonly
    If at any point a routine is not clear, don't hesitate to consult the <a href="namespacemembers.html">Procedures and Variables</a> page.
    \endhtmlonly
    \latexonly
    If at any point a routine is not clear, don't hesitate to consult the appendices.
    \endlatexonly
    
    \section tutorial_eq Getting the Equilibrium
    PB3D calculates stability of an equilibrium magnetic toroidal configuration.
    It is written in a modular way to isolate this main task from the actual code that was used to obtain the equilibrium configuration, and the format that this code may use.
    Currently, two formats are possible:
        - 3-D VMEC \cite hirshman1983vmec.
        - Axisymmetric HELENA \cite mikhailovskii1997optimization.

    VMEC is a general 3-D code built on an interesting idea: The poloidal coordinate \f$\theta\f$ is deformed in such as a way to make the Fourier basis that is used to describe the cylindrical coordinates \f$R(s,\theta,\varphi)\f$ and \f$Z(s,\theta,\varphi)\f$ as narrow as possible, where \f$s\f$ is the flux label (\f$s=\Psi_\text{tor}/\Psi_{\text{tor,edge}}\f$, the normalized toroidal flux) and \f$\varphi\f$ is the regular cylindrical angle (\ref fnt1 "1"). This is done by introducing a deformation factor \f$\lambda(s,\theta,\varphi)\f$ defined so that the straight-field line coordinate \f$\theta_\text{F}\f$ is given by \f$\theta_\text{F} = \theta + \lambda \f$.

    The fact that \f$R\f$, \f$Z\f$ and \f$\lambda\f$ are given as a double Fourier series in \f$\theta\f$ and \f$\zeta\f$ ensures that the angular derivatives can be done analytically. This property is used in PB3D. Apart from that, the flux functions that describe the profiles on the flux surfaces, the rotational transform \f$\iota(s)\f$ and pressure \f$p(s)\f$, are also returned.

    For HELENA, the situation is different, as this code considers axisymmetric equilibria only.
    Its output consists, aside from flux functions safety factor \f$q(\psi)\f$, pressure \f$p(\psi)\f$ and toroidal covariant magnetic field \f$F(\psi)\f$, where \f$\psi=\Psi_\text{pol}/\Psi_{\text{pol,edge}}\f$ is the normalized poloidal flux, of a 2-D table for the upper metric factors \f$h^{\psi\psi}\f$ and \f$h^{\psi\theta}\f$ and the lower metric factor \f$g_{\zeta\zeta}\f$.
    HELENA uses the same angular coordinates \f$\theta\f$ and \f$\zeta = -\varphi\f$ as PB3D (see \ref fnt1 "1").

    \note The modular nature of PB3D enables easy integration of other equilibrium codes, if necessary.

    To get the appropriate equilibrium code results into PB3D, it suffices to place the output files in the same directory in which PB3D is run:
        - for HELENA, it is a text file, corresponding to the mapping file (<tt>fort.12</tt>),
        - for VMEC, it is the so-called <tt>wout</tt> file,

    and to specify their names as the second input argument, the first one being the file with user inputs (see \ref tutorial_inputs).

    \note For VMEC, the <tt>wout</tt> file has to be in NetCDF format, and its name has to end in <tt>.nc</tt>, because PB3D recognizes a VMEC file by this extension (see open_input()). The <tt>.txt</tt> format of VMEC is not supported.

    In any case, if you forget how to run the PB3D (or POST) programs, you can always just type
    \code ./PB3D --help \endcode
    or even just
    \code ./PB3D \endcode

    \subsection tutorial_eq_helena Patching HELENA
    PB3D needs a HELENA that is changed in three places from the usual version. All three are in the subroutine \c mapping, which writes the mapping file <tt>fort.12</tt>.
        -# HELENA writes \c IAS after \c JS0 and \c B0 after \c RAXIS. Some versions do not write these by default. If yours is one of them, change
        \code write(nmap,8) JS0 \endcode
        to
        \code write(nmap,8) JS0, IAS \endcode
        and
        \code write(nmap,8) RAXIS \endcode
        to
        \code write(nmap,8) RAXIS, B0 \endcode
        Without them, PB3D stops with the error "Failed to read HELENA output file".
        -# HELENA writes the mapping file with 16 significant digits. HELENA's own formats write 8. Change the formats of the mapping output from \c E16.8 to \c E24.16:
        \code
    6 FORMAT(4E24.16)
    7 FORMAT(2E24.16)
    9 FORMAT(E24.16)
        \endcode
        (format 11, of the profile file <tt>fort.21</tt>, may change the same way; PB3D does not read that file). PB3D reads the file in free format, so it reads both versions without any change.
        -# HELENA is compiled in double precision, for example with <tt>gfortran -fdefault-real-8 -fdefault-double-8</tt>. In single precision, 16 written digits do not help.

    Then recompile and re-run HELENA.

    The 16 digits are not a claim about the accuracy of the equilibrium. HELENA's own discretisation error changes smoothly from one poloidal point to the next, but rounding to 8 digits adds an independent error of up to \f$5\times10^{-8}\f$ at each point. PB3D takes up to three derivatives in the poloidal angle \f$\chi\f$, which multiply such an error by about \f$1/h^3\f$, with \f$h\f$ the spacing of the poloidal points. With 8 digits, the eigenvalue can be off by per cents at low \f$n\f$ on fine mappings: with a free boundary at \f$n = 5\f$ and 2049 poloidal points, by 2 % for a circular \f$\epsilon = 0.33\f$ case and by 50 % for a pedestal case (PB3D issue 65). With 16 digits, the same cases converge smoothly in the number of poloidal points. Most of the error comes from the poloidal grid itself: HELENA sets it exactly to \f$\chi_j = \pi (j-1)/(N_\chi-1)\f$ (or \f$2\pi (j-1)/N_\chi\f$ without up-down symmetry), and 8 digits round it.

    \note PB3D checks this when it reads the file (see read_hel() and chi_h_rounding()). If the poloidal grid in the file differs from HELENA's exact grid by between \f$10^{-12}\f$ and \f$10^{-6}\f$, PB3D warns that the mapping file has too few digits and refers to this section. The run continues, because the error is small at higher \f$n\f$ and on coarse mappings (0.1 % or less at \f$n \geq 10\f$ with 1025 poloidal points), but the eigenvalue should not be trusted at low \f$n\f$ on fine mappings.



    \section tutorial_test_eq Checking the equilibrium (optional)
    It is always important that the equilibrium has been properly calculated, well-converged, etc.

    It is possible to use PB3D to check this. This will be quickly described in this optional section.
    
    The checks described below are only possible with the code compiled in the debug version, which is the CMake option <tt>-DPB3D_ENABLE_DEBUG=ON</tt> (see \ref page_installation).
    Using the debug version, the tests can be triggered using the \c --test option when running PB3D (or POST), and using the interactive user interface.

    They generally output HDF5 files that can be read with ParaView or VisIt to check the difference between two plots.
    After having a look in the code (search for \c ltest), this should be self-explanatory.

    \note Most of the checks are only useful for developers. However, the tests on \c B_F and on the pressure balance consistency are vital.
    
    <table>
    <caption id="tutorial_test_eq_tab">Table 1. the most important tests</caption>
    <tr>
        <th> tests </th><th> purpose </th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td> calculation of \c g_V </td><td> Check whether equilibrium metric coefficients calculated for VMEC equilibria through application of transformation matrices match with a direct implementation of the VMEC metric coefficients. Note that the reason why the transformation matrices are used, is that derivatives can be easily calculated as well, with high precision. </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td> calculation of \c jac_V </td><td> Similar to the test on the calculation of \c g_V, but this time for the Jacobian of VMEC coordinates. </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td> consistency of HELENA metric factors </td><td> Check whether the metric factors returned by HELENA are consistent with the ones calculated from the flux quantities, \f$R\f$ and \f$Z\f$. Basically, this is a measure for the accuracy with which the pressure balance is satisfied. </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td> harmonic content of HELENA output </td><td> Analyze harmonic content of HELENA variables. </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td> calculation of <tt>D1 D2 h_H</tt> </td><td> Check whether the cross-derivatives are correctly and accurately calculated. </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td> calculation of \c T_EF </td><td> Check is \f$\mathcal{T}_\text{E}^\text{F}\f$ complies with the (unpublished) theory. </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td> calculation of \c jac_F </td><td> Similar to the test on the calculation of \c jac_V, but this time for the Jacobian of Flux coordinates. </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td> calculation of \c B_F </td><td> Similar to the test on the calculation of \c jac_F, but this time for the covariant components and magnitude of the magnetic field in Flux coordinates. </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td> consistency of pressure balance </td><td> Check how well the pressure balance is satisfied. This is a very crucial requirement for the correct calculation of stability: If the pressure balance is not satisfied up to high precision, there is not much for PB3D to do. </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td> calculation of \c DU </td><td> Check how well the poloidal derivative of the geodesical component of the minimizing perturbation, \f$U\f$, is calculated by comparing it to the numerical derivative. </td>
    </tr>
    </table>
    

    For the specific case of VMEC equilibria, finally, there is the additional \c --plot_VMEC_modes option that produces outputs of the harmonics of the different quantities: \f$R\f$, \f$Z\f$, \f$\lambda\f$, \f$\mathcal{J}\f$ and \f$\vec{B}\f$.

    It is very important that these are well-converged, as the internal consistency depends strongly on this. Therefore, if this is not the case, be prepared to receive unaccurate stability results.

    

    \section tutorial_inputs Setting Up the Input File
    Now that we have the equilibrium files, it is time to have a look at the input file.
    
    \includelineno PB3D.input
    
    Have a look:
        - \c min_n_par_X is the number of points in the parallel grid of every field line at the first Richardson level.
        - These points range from \c min_par_X to \c max_par_X, in units of \f$\pi\f$.
        - How the field lines are chosen depends on \c alpha_style.
        This file sets <tt>alpha_style = 2</tt>, which is also the default for VMEC: PB3D uses \c n_alpha field lines, here 64, with equally spaced labels, and follows each of them over one poloidal turn.
        PB3D sets \c min_par_X and \c max_par_X to 0 and 2 if their difference is not 2 (see \ref page_inputs); in this file, they are already 0 and 2.
        - With <tt>alpha_style = 1</tt>, the default for HELENA, PB3D follows the single field line with label \c alpha, and the parallel range from \c min_par_X to \c max_par_X can span several poloidal turns.
        For 3-D equilibria, the number of field lines \c n_alpha (<tt>alpha_style = 2</tt>) or the parallel range (<tt>alpha_style = 1</tt>), together with \c min_n_par_X, has to be increased until convergence is reached \cite Weyens2017PB3D.
        - \c n_r_sol is the number of points in the solution grid, that ranges from \c min_r_sol to \c max_r_sol in the PB3D normal coordinate (see num_vars.use_pol_flux_f).
        - \c n_mod_X is the number of Fourier harmonics that are kept, with \c prim_X the primary mode number.
        With the poloidal flux as normal coordinate (the default), \c prim_X is the toroidal mode number \f$n\f$ and the harmonics are poloidal mode numbers \f$m\f$.
        - \c BC_style(1:2) indicates the style with which the boundary conditions are implemented deep in the plasma and on the plasma edge.
        - \c max_tot_mem is a memory limit in MB: PB3D divides its work into jobs so that its own estimate of the memory it needs stays below this limit.
        The estimate is larger than the memory the run really uses; section \ref tutorial_PB3D_results gives the numbers for this example.
        The estimate includes the memory that every process holds before the work starts (MPI, the libraries and the input), about 0.1 GB on macOS and 0.3 GB on Linux per process.
        When the limit is too low for the parts of the calculation that cannot be divided, PB3D stops at the start and gives the number of MB it needs.
        - \c max_it_rich is the number of Richardson levels (see the extra options below); with 1, PB3D does not extrapolate.
        - \c use_normalization indicates whether normalization is used (recommended), but it is useful to turn this off for debugging.

    \note In this file, <tt>BC_style = 1 2</tt> makes the plasma edge a free boundary: PB3D calculates the response of the vacuum outside the plasma and adds its energy to the perturbed potential energy (see \ref page_overview and \ref page_vacuum).
    <tt>BC_style(2) = 2</tt> is also the default.
    With <tt>BC_style = 1 1</tt>, the displacement is zero at the plasma edge, as if a conducting wall lay on the plasma surface, which makes it a fixed boundary.
    The inner end, deep in the plasma, only accepts <tt>BC_style(1) = 1</tt>.
    For VMEC, a free boundary needs <tt>alpha_style = 2</tt> with more than one field line, as PB3D then uses a 3-D boundary-element method on the points of the field lines on the plasma boundary; PB3D stops with an error for <tt>alpha_style = 1</tt>.
    The number of field lines decides how well this method resolves the toroidal variation of the vacuum potential, \f$e^{-in\zeta}\f$: there must be clearly more than \f$2n+1\f$ of them.
    Section \ref tutorial_PB3D_results shows how accurate 64 field lines with 100 points each are for this example.

    There are also some extra options listed (but not loaded in PB3D as they come after the end of the \c inputdata_PB3D block of the Fortran namelist) below:
    \snippetlineno PB3D.input some extra options for PB3D
    They include:
        - \c rich_restart_lvl is used to restart a simulation at a certain Richardson level. The Richardson level 1 refers to the first simulation, with the number of parallel points used in the calculation of line integrals along the magnetic field set by \c min_n_par_X (see \ref inputs_PB3D_file_tab). For every subsequent Richardson level, this number \f$n\f$ becomes \f$2n-1\f$, so that the grid spacing is halved. The results from these different levels are extrapolated to a theoretically infinitely fine grid. These operations are done in module rich_ops.
        - \c tol_rich is the tolerance at which Richardson extrapolation is considered to have converged.
        - \c tol_SLEPC is an array of tolerances used for the SLEPc eigenvalue solutions of the different Richardson levels.

    There are many, many more input parameters. A short description of these can be found in \ref page_inputs.



    \section tutorial_PB3D Running PB3D
    This tutorial provides a VMEC equilibrium file to experiment with: the \c cbm18 equilibrium of the benchmark for edge-localized modes of Ferraro et al. \cite ferraro2010ELMbenchmark, a nearly circular tokamak with a steep pressure pedestal at the edge, which is unstable to peeling-ballooning modes.
    The plasma ends at the foot of the pedestal, where the pressure gradient and the current density fall to zero, and outside it is vacuum.
    The file was made from the HELENA equilibrium of this plasma, with PB3D's option <tt>--export_HEL</tt> and VMEC++ on 639 flux surfaces; its safety factor agrees with that of HELENA to five digits.
    Lengths and fields are in SI units.
    Figure 1 shows the flux surfaces and the profiles of the pressure and the safety factor: the pressure falls from 25 kPa at \f$\psi = 0.75\f$ to 0.2 kPa at \f$\psi = 0.95\f$, where \f$\psi\f$ is the normalized poloidal flux.
    Some properties are given in table \ref tutorial_PB3D_tab "2".

    \image html tutorial_equilibrium.png "Figure 1: the equilibrium of this tutorial. Left: the flux surfaces in a poloidal cross-section, every 0.1 in the normalized poloidal flux, with the magnetic axis marked by a cross. Right: the pressure and the absolute value of the safety factor against the normalized poloidal flux." width=90%
    \image latex tutorial_equilibrium.png "Figure 1: the equilibrium of this tutorial. Left: the flux surfaces in a poloidal cross-section, every 0.1 in the normalized poloidal flux, with the magnetic axis marked by a cross. Right: the pressure and the absolute value of the safety factor against the normalized poloidal flux." width=\textwidth

