    </tr>
    </table>

    Put this equilibrium file, as well as the input file in a separate run directory; let's call it \c cbm18.

    In order to produce outputs, PB3D needs three sub-directories in the run directory, called <tt>Data/</tt>, <tt>Scripts/</tt> and <tt>Plots/</tt>.
        - In <tt>Data/</tt>, the data files for the external plots are stored, as well as the HDF5 plots (<tt>.h5</tt> and <tt>.xmf</tt> files) that can be read later in visualization software such as VisIt or ParaView.
        - In <tt>Scripts/</tt>, scripts are generated that use the data files from <tt>Data/</tt>.
        - In <tt>Plots/</tt>, the resulting external plots are located.
        In \ref output_plots, this is explained in more detail.

    If these directories are not present, you will receive an error message, so go ahead and create them.

    We can now run the code using \c mpirun. For example, with
    \code mpirun -np 4 ./PB3D PB3D.input wout.nc \endcode
    4 processes are used.
    With more than one process, the eigenvalue solver needs PETSc with a parallel direct solver such as MUMPS (see \ref page_faq); a single process can also be used, by running <tt>./PB3D PB3D.input wout.nc</tt> without \c mpirun.
    You can find the VMEC file <a href="https://pb3d.github.io/Examples/wout.nc">here</a>.
    The HELENA input file of the same plasma is <a href="https://pb3d.github.io/Examples/fort.10">here</a>; with a HELENA patched as described in \ref tutorial_eq_helena, it gives the mapping file that PB3D reads instead of <tt>wout.nc</tt>.

    \note On macOS, run PB3D and POST with <tt>MallocLargeCache=0</tt> in the environment, for example
    \code MallocLargeCache=0 ./PB3D PB3D.input wout.nc \endcode
    Without it, macOS keeps the memory that PB3D has freed, and PB3D can take about twice \c max_tot_mem; PB3D warns about this at the start.
    PB3D cannot set this variable itself, as it has to be set when the program starts.

    There are optional run-time options that can be triggered, such as <tt>--jump_to_sol</tt>, which can be used to easily change solution preferences, such as discretization order, etc.
    For an overview, see \ref inputs_PB3D_cmd; they are not treated here.

    You will see formatted output on the screen, with indentation according to the depth of a routine in the program.
    The same output is also written to the output file <tt>PB3D_out.txt</tt>, unformatted but still indented (see \ref output_file_log).

    The resulting eigenvalues can also be read in a separate output file, <tt>PB3D_out_EV_R_1.txt</tt> for the first Richardson level (see \ref output_file_EV).
    The other results are written to the HDF5 file <tt>PB3D_out.h5</tt>, which POST reads.

    Take a look at these output files and shuffle a bit with the input parameters if you want.
    For example, change \c n_mod_X to another value in order to get a different number of Fourier harmonics, or \c n_r_sol to change the number of normal grid points in the solution.
    The solution should not change a lot if you have enough harmonics and normal grid points.

    \note For VMEC, the magnetic axis is a bit problematic. It is necessary, therefore, to choose \c min_r_sol to be slightly larger than 0; 0.01 for example.

    \subsection tutorial_PB3D_results What to expect
    On one core of a laptop (Apple M3 Pro, with <tt>MallocLargeCache=0</tt>), this run takes about 2.5 minutes, and its memory use peaks at 4.0 GB, below the limit \c max_tot_mem of 8000 MB.
    Most of the time goes into the plasma quantities on the 64 field lines, which PB3D divides into 25 equilibrium jobs; the vacuum takes 10 s.
    This input file needs a \c max_tot_mem of at least 5.7 GB on this laptop with one process, and about 7.4 GB on Linux with 4 processes: the vacuum matrices exist in every equilibrium job and in the solution driver, and the integrated perturbation quantities in the solution driver, which cannot be divided into jobs, and every process holds memory of its own.
    With a lower limit, PB3D stops at the start and gives the number it needs.

    PB3D gives the eigenvalues \f$\lambda = \omega^2/\omega_A^2\f$, normalized to the square of the Alfvén frequency \f$\omega_A\f$ that the header of <tt>PB3D_out_EV_R_1.txt</tt> gives.
    For this input file, the first eigenvalue should be close to \f$\lambda = -0.0499\f$.
    A negative eigenvalue belongs to an unstable mode, with the growth rate \f$\gamma = \omega_A\sqrt{-\lambda}\f$, here \f$0.22\,\omega_A\f$.

    Figure 2 shows the eigenvector: the amplitudes of the poloidal Fourier harmonics \f$X_m\f$ of the normal displacement \f$X = \vec{\xi}\cdot\nabla\psi\f$.
    Each harmonic peaks close to the flux surface on which it is resonant, where \f$n|q| = m\f$; the upper axis of the figure gives \f$n|q|\f$.
    The largest harmonics, \f$m = 18\f$ to \f$23\f$, lie where the pressure falls most steeply.
    At the plasma boundary, where \f$n|q| = 30.4\f$, the harmonic \f$m = 31\f$, which would be resonant just outside the plasma, still has 0.27 of the largest amplitude, so that the mode moves the boundary.

    \image html tutorial_harmonics.png "Figure 2: amplitudes of the poloidal Fourier harmonics of the normal displacement of the first eigenvector, for n = 10, divided by the largest value. The upper axis gives n|q|; each harmonic m peaks close to where n|q| = m." width=85%
    \image latex tutorial_harmonics.png "Figure 2: amplitudes of the poloidal Fourier harmonics of the normal displacement of the first eigenvector, for n = 10, divided by the largest value. The upper axis gives n|q|; each harmonic m peaks close to where n|q| = m." width=\textwidth

    Figure 3 shows the same eigenvector in the poloidal plane \f$\zeta = 0\f$, and the potential of the perturbed magnetic field in the vacuum outside the plasma.
    The displacement is largest on the outboard side, where the curvature of the field lines makes the pressure gradient destabilizing: the mode balloons.
    Outside the plasma, the vacuum potential falls off over a distance comparable to the distance between its maxima along the boundary.
    Figure 4 shows the displacement on the whole plasma boundary: its maxima and minima follow the field lines, with 10 periods around the torus, and it is largest on the outboard side.
    POST computes both figures (see \ref tutorial_POST); they were plotted with matplotlib from the HDF5 files that POST writes in <tt>Data/</tt>.

    \image html tutorial_mode.png "Figure 3: real part of the first eigenvector in the poloidal plane at zeta = 0. Left: the normal displacement X inside the plasma; the solution range starts at a normalized poloidal flux of 0.3. Right: the vacuum potential outside the plasma. Both in arbitrary units." width=90%
    \image latex tutorial_mode.png "Figure 3: real part of the first eigenvector in the poloidal plane at zeta = 0. Left: the normal displacement X inside the plasma; the solution range starts at a normalized poloidal flux of 0.3. Right: the vacuum potential outside the plasma. Both in arbitrary units." width=\textwidth
    \image html tutorial_mode_3d.png "Figure 4: real part of the normal displacement X of the first eigenvector on the plasma boundary, over the whole torus (red positive, blue negative)." width=75%
    \image latex tutorial_mode_3d.png "Figure 4: real part of the normal displacement X of the first eigenvector on the plasma boundary, over the whole torus (red positive, blue negative)." width=0.8\textwidth

    Table \ref tutorial_PB3D_res_tab "3" shows how this eigenvalue changes when the input file is changed.
    Every row changes one thing, and raises \c max_tot_mem where the run needs more.
    The rows marked HELENA use the HELENA mapping of the same plasma, without the lines with \c alpha_style and \c n_alpha (\ref fnt3 "3"); for HELENA, PB3D computes the vacuum with an axisymmetric method (see \ref page_vacuum).
