    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td> HELENA, <tt>BC_style = 1 1</tt> </td><td> \f$-0.04028\f$ </td><td> 3 s </td><td> 1.0 GB </td>
    </tr>
    </table>

    The times varied by about 15 % between repeated runs of the same input file, and more while other programs ran on the laptop.
    The table shows the following.
        - With 100 points on each field line, the points along the lines limit the accuracy of the vacuum.
        200 points per line move the eigenvalue by 0.8 %, to within 0.1 % of the HELENA value, while 128 field lines instead of 64 move it by only 0.05 %.
        - With 30 Fourier harmonics instead of 20, the free-boundary eigenvalue on the HELENA mapping is 0.2 % more negative; the fixed-boundary eigenvalue on the HELENA mapping changes by less than \f$10^{-4}\f$ of its value.
        - With the vacuum, the eigenvalue is 24 % more negative than with a conducting wall on the plasma surface, so the growth rate is 11 % larger.
        - \c min_r_sol = 0.3 lies far enough inward. On the HELENA mapping, with the same spacing of the radial points, the free-boundary eigenvalue changes by \f$10^{-5}\f$ of its value when the solution range starts at 0.1 instead. When it starts at 0.5, the range cuts off the inner part of the eigenfunction, and the eigenvalue is 0.2 % less negative.
        - The radial grid is fine enough: with 1120 points instead of 400, the HELENA eigenvalue with a free boundary changes by 0.04 %.


    \section tutorial_scripts Run Script Tools

    PB3D comes with a bunch of extra run tools, which can greatly improve user experience.
    
    \note These tools are in a separate git repository, \c <a href="https://github.com/ToonWeyens/PB3D_tools">PB3D_tools</a>.

    In table \ref tutorial_scripts_tab "4", an overview is given:
    <table>
    <caption id="tutorial_scripts_tab">Table 4. extra tools</caption>
    <tr>
        <td> <tt>run</tt> </td> <td> Run script that can be used for PB3D and POST with an extensive range of features including
            - the possibility to do a parameter scan for a variety of input variables
            - automatic support for schedulers such as PBS and SLURM
            - support for running on local folders when on a HPC cluster
            - etc...

        \note Use of this runscript is recommended, as it will make your life *much* easier, especially when you are working on a cluster.
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td> <tt>extract_jobs_info</tt>  </td> <td> Extracts the eigenvalues from the results of a parameter scan produced by <tt>run</tt> with input variable modifications. </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td> <tt>inspect_SLURM_jobs</tt>  </td> <td> Finds the directories of all the jobs currently running in SLURM and in every directory, it gives the tail of <tt>PB3D.out</tt>. The user can decide to cancel the job. </td>
    </tr>
    </table>


    \section tutorial_POST Post-processing With POST

    POST provides a way to visualize the results from PB3D.
    Many options are possible, and they can be activated using <tt>plot_</tt> flags (see \ref inputs_POST_file).

    An example is given in <tt>POST.input</tt>
    \includelineno POST.input
    It can be seen that apart from the <tt>plot_</tt> flags, there are also
        - \c min_r_plot and \c max_r_plot, which are the direct equivalent of \c min_r_sol and \c max_r_sol in PB3D.
        - \c min_theta_plot, \c max_theta_plot and \c n_theta_plot which indicate the range of the poloidal variable that is plot, as well as the number of points. There is an equivalent for the toroidal variable.
        - \c POST_style that allows the user to change between different styles, such as whether the output grid is chosen to fill the torus in a regular way, for example, or whether it follows the PB3D equilibrium grid.
        - \c plot_grid_style, subsequently, can be used to change the way in which these grids are produced. For example, they could be changed to slab plots or plots with a straightened toroidal angle.
        - \c pert_mult_factor_POST can be used to deform the output grids by the perturbation itself.

    Also here, there are also some extra options listed below:
    \snippetlineno POST.input some extra options for POST
    They include:
        - \c ex_plot_style to indicate which program is to be used for external plotting. Changing it to 2 would engage Bokeh and Mayavi.
        - \c PB3D_rich_lvl can be optionally employed to specify explicitly which Richardson level to use for the post-processing.
    
    All of these options are explained in \ref inputs_POST_file.
    Again, there are also run-time options that can be found in \ref inputs_POST_cmd.
    
    Now, run the POST program, for example using 4 MPI processes:
    \code mpirun -np 4 ./POST POST.input PB3D_out.h5 \endcode
    For this example, POST takes about half a minute on one core.

    An interesting output is the energy reconstruction, which is activated with \c plot_E_rec.
    This calculation reinserts the eigenvector into the terms of the perturbed potential energy and of the kinetic energy, in order to check whether the Rayleigh quotient still holds, and to see which terms are dominant and which ones are less important.
    The integrated terms are also stored in a separate file, <tt>POST_out_EN_R[lvl].txt</tt> for Richardson level <tt>[lvl]</tt> (see \ref output_file_E_rec).

    Figure 5 shows these terms for this example, divided by the kinetic energy, so that they add up to the eigenvalue: their sum, \f$-0.04984\f$, differs from the eigenvalue \f$-0.04987\f$ by \f$7\times10^{-4}\f$ of its value.
    The normal ballooning term, which holds the pressure gradient and the normal curvature of the field lines, drives the mode most, and the line bending terms stabilize it.
    The figure also shows the terms of the fixed-boundary run (<tt>BC_style = 1 1</tt>).
    The vacuum term itself is small and positive, \f$+0.005\f$.
    The free boundary makes the mode more unstable because the eigenvector changes: with the free boundary, the normal ballooning term is more negative by 0.025 and the normal kink term, which holds the parallel current, by 0.012, while the line bending terms grow by 0.027.

    \image html tutorial_energy.png "Figure 5: terms of the potential energy of the first eigenvector, divided by its kinetic energy, for the free boundary of this tutorial and for a fixed boundary (POST, plot_E_rec)." width=80%
    \image latex tutorial_energy.png "Figure 5: terms of the potential energy of the first eigenvector, divided by its kinetic energy, for the free boundary of this tutorial and for a fixed boundary (POST, plot_E_rec)." width=0.9\textwidth

    Because this example has a free boundary, POST also plots the vacuum potential of the eigenmode outside the plasma (\c plot_vac_pot, which is set by default), in the files <tt>Data/1_vac_pot_RE.xmf</tt> and <tt>Data/1_vac_pot_IM.xmf</tt> for its real and imaginary parts; <tt>Data/vac_in_plasma.xmf</tt> marks the points of the plot that lie inside the plasma.
    After a fixed-boundary run, POST switches this plot off.

    If <tt>--do_execute_command_line</tt> was not explicitly used, the actual external plot programs are not used yet, but their usage is given in a shell commands script file (see \ref output_file_shell).
    Run this shell with
    \code ./POST_shell_commands.sh \endcode

    If everything is okay, you should now see output in the <tt>Plots/</tt> folder.
    If an error occurs, please verify whether you have appropriate versions of the external plot programs (i.e. GnuPlot, Bokeh, ...)

    Also, open some of the <tt>.xmf</tt> files in <tt>Data/</tt> in VisIt or ParaView to explore these plots.
    
    To finish this tutorial, feel free to adjust some input parameters.
    For example, you can change the output grid to become 3-D by setting \c n_zeta_plot and \c max_zeta_plot, or you could deform it using \c pert_mult_factor_POST.
    Figures 3 and 4 were made this way, each from a separate POST run in which only \c plot_sol_xi is set, and \c plot_vac_pot for figure 3:
        - figure 3 with <tt>n_theta_plot = 401</tt>, and the vacuum potential on a finer box with <tt>min_Rvac_plot = 1.9</tt>, <tt>max_Rvac_plot = 5.9</tt>, <tt>min_Zvac_plot = -1.9</tt>, <tt>max_Zvac_plot = 1.9</tt> and <tt>n_vac_plot = 321 305</tt> (2 minutes on one core);
        - figure 4 with <tt>min_r_plot = 0.99</tt>, <tt>n_theta_plot = 201</tt>, <tt>n_zeta_plot = 241</tt>, <tt>max_zeta_plot = 2.0</tt> and without the vacuum potential, which gives the outermost flux surfaces over the whole torus (20 s).

    \note
        -# \anchor fnt1 The cylindrical angle \f$\varphi\f$ is often the inverse of the one used in nuclear fusion research, as it can lead to left-handed coordinate systems. In VMEC, it is opted to use it anyway, so it has a left-handed coordinate system. The poloidal angle is chosen to be anticlockwise if you look at a cross-section of the plasma that lies to the right of the \f$Z\f$-axis with the \f$Z\f$-axis pointing up, as in PB3D. In PB3D, however, the toroidal angle \f$\zeta = - \varphi\f$ is chosen as the inverse of the cylindrical angle, so it is right-handed.
        -# \anchor fnt2 In the VMEC file of this tutorial, the toroidal flux and the rotational transform are both negative, as in the HELENA equilibrium it was made from, so that \f$q\f$ is negative.
        -# \anchor fnt3 HELENA equilibria are axisymmetric, so PB3D needs only one field line for them. With <tt>n_alpha = 64</tt> in the input file, PB3D warns about this, but it still uses 64 field lines, and it then stops because its memory limit \c max_tot_mem is too low.

    \example PB3D.input Example PB3D input.
    \example POST.input Example POST input.
*/
